<https://culturalcartography.net/names/wilhelm-olbers/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Wilhelm Olbers" ;
    dcterms:description "The astronomer Olbers (who always kept a 'day job' in medicine, and is sometimes known as Heinrich Wilhelm Olbers) and the great mathematician and astronomer Gauss were regular correspondents. Encke told him (and Bessel and Gauss) when he made his momentous discovery of comets with short orbital periods. Struck by the self-taught Bessel's prodigious talents, Olbers persuaded him to drop the security of his apprenticeship in commerce, and become an astronomer instead."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://scihi.org/heinrich-olbers-and-the-olbers-paradox/>,
        <https://www.britannica.com/biography/Wilhelm-Olbers> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q75828 ;
    skos:altLabel "Heinrich Wilhelm Olbers" ;
    foaf:familyName "Olbers" ;
    foaf:givenName "Wilhelm" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/wilhelm-olbers/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Wilhelm_Olbers> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/carl-friedrich-gauss/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/franz-xaver-von-zach/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/friedrich-bessel/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/johann-franz-encke/#person> ;
    foaf:name "Wilhelm Olbers" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "Arbergen, Germany" ;
    wkw:datesText "1758 (Arbergen, Germany) – 1840 (Bremen)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "Bremen" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Olbers, Wilhelm" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 3965 ;
    schema:birthDate "1758"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1840"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/astronomer/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/willard-gibbs/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Willard Gibbs" ;
    dcterms:description "Gibbs helped found chemical thermodynamics, and did fundamental work on vector analysis (sparked by reading Maxwell), while his work in theoretical physics paved the way for quantum mechanics. Maxwell, one of the first to recognise Gibbs' genius, later made and sent him a plaster model. Although Gibbs, self-contained, rarely travelled, his mailing-list of 300 prominent scientists included his correspondents Peirce, Rayleigh and Ostwald (who translated his work). In Germany he sat in on lectures by Kirchhoff, Helmholtz, Bunsen and Magnus (influences all); however it's not known whether he actually met them."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.britannica.com/biography/J-Willard-Gibbs>,
        <https://www.uh.edu/engines/epi119.htm> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q153243 ;
    skos:altLabel "Josiah Willard Gibbs;J. Willard Gibbs" ;
    foaf:familyName "Gibbs" ;
    foaf:givenName "Willard" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/willard-gibbs/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josiah_Willard_Gibbs> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/charles-sanders-peirce/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/james-clerk-maxwell/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/lord-rayleigh/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/wilhelm-ostwald/#person> ;
    foaf:name "Willard Gibbs" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "New Haven, Conn." ;
    wkw:datesText "1839 (New Haven, Conn.) – 1903 (New Haven)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "New Haven" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Gibbs, Willard" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 3537 ;
    schema:birthDate "1839"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1903"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/chemist/#concept>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/mathematician/#concept>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/physicist/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/william-beckford/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "William Beckford" ;
    dcterms:description "Constable visited the extremely wealthy Beckford to study his painting collection (including works by Rembrandt, Titian, Raphael, Velazquez and Claude). Turner also visited, commissioned by Beckford. Beckford himself had been taught drawing as a child by Alexander Cozens, and met Voltaire on his first visit abroad. He became West's patron, matching the king's thousand-pound annuity. Beckford's own story that as a young child he was taught music by Mozart (only 3 years older) is widely doubted. His best-known work, the gothic novel 'Vathek', written in French rather than English, has influenced French as much as English literature."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://rictornorton.co.uk/beckfor1.htm>,
        <https://www.britannica.com/biography/William-Beckford-British-writer> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q361026 ;
    foaf:familyName "Beckford" ;
    foaf:givenName "William" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/william-beckford/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Thomas_Beckford> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/benjamin-west/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/j-m-w-turner/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/john-constable/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/voltaire/#person> ;
    foaf:name "William Beckford" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "London" ;
    wkw:datesText "1760 (London) – 1844 (Bath, England)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "Bath, England" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Beckford, William" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 3158 ;
    schema:birthDate "1760"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1844"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/writer/#concept> .

