<https://culturalcartography.net/names/giovanni-battista-morgagni/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Giovanni Battista Morgagni" ;
    dcterms:description "Morgagni was a founder of modern pathology; his magnum opus, immensely influential, was translated into the major European languages. Valsalva was one of his teachers — their friendship was critical to Morgagni's career, Morgagni assisting then succeeding him, and editing his works. Boerhaave, Réaumur and Haller (who called him his master) were among his correspondents, and Scarpa probably the most notable of his students. Incidentally, Lorenzo da Ponte attended his last series of lectures and later told 'many charming stories' about him; suggesting (though it is unclear) a genuine mutual connection."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.britannica.com/biography/Giovanni-Battista-Morgagni>,
        <https://www.uh.edu/engines/epi358.htm> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q511841 ;
    foaf:familyName "Morgagni" ;
    foaf:givenName "Giovanni Battista" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/giovanni-battista-morgagni/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Battista_Morgagni> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/albrecht-von-haller/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/antonio-maria-valsalva/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/antonio-scarpa/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/herman-boerhaave/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/rene-antoine-de-reaumur/#person> ;
    foaf:name "Giovanni Battista Morgagni" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "Forlì, Italy" ;
    wkw:datesText "1682 (Forlì, Italy) – 1771 (Padua)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "Padua" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Morgagni, Giovannibattista" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 3916 ;
    schema:birthDate "1682"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1771"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/anatomist/#concept>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/pathologist/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/giovanni-paisiello/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Giovanni Paisiello" ;
    dcterms:description "Durante taught Paisiello, and da Ponte provided him with a libretto. He met Mozart in Vienna; Michael Kelly recounted how he dined with both at Mozart's house, 'and often afterwards enjoyed their company together.' Vigée le Brun painted his portrait."@en ;
