<https://culturalcartography.net/names/david-ricardo/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "David Ricardo" ;
    dcterms:description "Ricardo's influence on the science of economics derives from barely a dozen years' work. Ricardo was the most notable of the circle around Bentham, supporters of his utilitarian ideas. Wealthy from his stock-dealing, he was persuaded to publish his most important work by James Mill, a particularly close friend (John Stuart Mill, his precocious son, went for walks with their frequent visitor, and commented on Ricardo's lack of abrasive pushiness, drawing a contrast with his father). Malthus, a friend and close associate of Ricardo, provided the stimulation of his oppositional views. De Quincey was a disciple, but it seems they never met."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.d.umn.edu/cla/faculty/jhamlin/4111/Ricardo/David%20Ricardo.htm> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q160270 ;
    foaf:familyName "Ricardo" ;
    foaf:givenName "David" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/david-ricardo/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Ricardo> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/james-mill/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/jeremy-bentham/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/john-stuart-mill/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/maria-edgeworth/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/robert-owen/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/thomas-malthus/#person> ;
    foaf:name "David Ricardo" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "London" ;
    wkw:datesText "1772 (London) – 1823 (Gatcombe, England)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "Gatcombe, England" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Ricardo, David" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 4078 ;
    schema:birthDate "1772"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1823"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/economist/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/derek-jarman/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Derek Jarman" ;
    dcterms:description "Jarman was an influential and in many ways radical film-maker, rather too easily pigeonholed by issues of sexuality. Russell, whose adventurous film-making was a formative influence on Jarman, employed him as a set-designer. Betjeman showed him around churches, Eno wrote soundtracks for him, and Houédard helped him get the Latin right for his first major film, Sebastiane. Bowie was a friend, Mapplethorpe briefly a lover (with much subsequent mutual rancour)."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.queerculturalcenter.org/Pages/Jarman/JarmanBio.html>,
        <https://www.screenonline.org.uk/people/id/460999/>,
        <https://www.sensesofcinema.com/2007/great-directors/jarman/> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q282787 ;
    foaf:familyName "Jarman" ;
    foaf:givenName "Derek" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/derek-jarman/> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/brian-eno/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/david-bowie/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/dom-sylvester-houedard/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/john-betjeman/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/ken-russell/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/robert-mapplethorpe/#person> ;
    foaf:name "Derek Jarman" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "Northwood, England" ;
    wkw:datesText "1942 (Northwood, England) – 1994 (London)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "London" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Jarman, Derek" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 4651 ;
    schema:birthDate "1942"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1994"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/film-maker/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/ed-kienholz/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Ed Kienholz" ;
    dcterms:description "Kienholz is easily underestimated – online sources, and pictures in general, don't readily do justice to the way that his most notable tableaux and installations (e.g. Five Car Stud, Roxy's, the Beanery, the State Hospital, Portable War Memorial, The Illegal Operation, Back Seat Dodge) represent unusually powerful images of the seamy underside and questionable mores of his time. Irwin, Bell, Berman and Ruscha were among the close-knit group associated with the important L.A. gallery Kienholz co-founded. Tinguely collaborated with him, while Klein honoured him (alone among his American artist friends) with a piece of his Void."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.nytimes.com/1996/03/01/arts/art-review-edward-kienholz-s-great-big-assault.html> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q583959 ;
    foaf:familyName "Kienholz" ;
    foaf:givenName "Ed" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/ed-kienholz/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Kienholz> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/ed-ruscha/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/jean-tinguely/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/larry-bell/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/robert-irwin/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/wallace-berman/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/yves-klein/#person> ;
    foaf:name "Ed Kienholz" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "Fairfield, Wash." ;
    wkw:datesText "1927 (Fairfield, Wash.) – 1994 (Hope, Idaho)" ;
    wkw:deathPlaceText "Hope, Idaho" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Kienholz, Ed" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 5682 ;
    schema:birthDate "1927"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:deathDate "1994"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/artist/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/ed-ruscha/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Ed Ruscha" ;
    dcterms:description "Ruscha's paintings, books and other works are often said to mark him out as having a distinctively west-coast sensibility, though his influence spread much further. Irwin was strongly influential as a teacher, and later a stable-mate (along with Berman, and Ruscha's fellow-student Bell) at the Ferus Gallery, co-founded by Kienholz. Hockney was a friend, and similarly playful observer of west coast ways; Wiener, a colleague and good acquaintance, collaborated on a bookwork; Baldessari also collaborated. Ruscha's admirer J. G. Ballard (the respect was mutual) wrote that he had 'the coolest gaze in American art.'"@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://edruscha.com/about-ed-ruscha/>,
        <https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2013/07/01/ed-ruschas-l-a>,
        <https://www.theguardian.com/artanddesign/2018/jun/12/ed-ruscha-interview-america-decline-course-of-empire-national-gallery> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q430967 ;
    foaf:familyName "Ruscha" ;
    foaf:givenName "Ed" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/ed-ruscha/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Ruscha> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/david-hockney/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/ed-kienholz/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/john-baldessari/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/lawrence-wiener/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/robert-irwin/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/wallace-berman/#person> ;
    foaf:name "Ed Ruscha" ;
    wkw:birthPlaceText "Omaha, Nebr." ;
    wkw:datesText "1937 (Omaha, Nebr.) –" ;
    wkw:sortKey "Ruscha, Ed" ;
    wkw:status "bio" ;
    wkw:wordpressId 5709 ;
    schema:birthDate "1937"^^xsd:gYear ;
    schema:hasOccupation <https://culturalcartography.net/professions/artist/#concept> .

<https://culturalcartography.net/names/edith-piaf/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Édith Piaf" ;
    dcterms:description "Cocteau was a close friend: he wrote a short play for her, and famously died deeply affected a few hours after hearing of her own death. Montand was a protégé and lover. Captivated by his music, she helped Yupanqui in his career, and gave Aznavour (her secretary, driver and confidant — who also wrote for her) his big break. Chaplin was a friend, and Dietrich a close one (she was witness at Piaf's first wedding); no substantial evidence seems to exist to support rumours that they may also have been lovers."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.last.fm/music/Édith+Piaf/+wiki> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q1631 ;
    foaf:familyName "Piaf" ;
    foaf:givenName "Édith" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/edith-piaf/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Édith_Piaf> ;
