<https://culturalcartography.net/names/christopher-logue/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Christopher Logue" ;
    dcterms:description "Logue, better-known in Britain than elsewhere (his translation of the Iliad is celebrated), was a socially-engaged poet with a strong suit in wry satire. Trocchi's Paris-based magazine 'Merlin' published early work of Logue's; Trocchi also saved him from suicide. Logue wrote a pornographic novel for Girodias, and hung out with Beckett and Miller. He appeared in a fim of Gilliam's as a spaghetti-eating maniac. Wesker and he were jailed together for civil disobedience. He said that Donleavy held grudges obsessively, and had nothing but books about copyright on his bookshelves."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.theguardian.com/books/2006/jan/22/poetry.features>,
        <https://www.theguardian.com/books/2011/dec/03/christopher-logue> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q542468 ;
    foaf:familyName "Logue" ;
    foaf:givenName "Christopher" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/christopher-logue/>,
