<https://culturalcartography.net/names/felix-vicq-dazyr/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Félix Vicq d'Azyr" ;
    dcterms:description "Daubenton taught and strongly influenced him. He was a family friend of Fourcroy's, and encouraged him to study medicine (they became fellow-students). Blumenbach was an important collaborator — they effectively invented comparative anatomy as a systematic science. D'Alembert gave him an Italian work about public health; he got it published in French, and helped change public opinion about the unhealthiness of burial-grounds. Turgot, Condorcet and Lavoisier were all friends; brought in by Turgot, his radical approach to epidemic cattle-plague (not to mention his pioneering of epidemiology) is what really made his name."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.nature.com/articles/161634b0>,
        <https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Felix-Vicq-d%27Azyr%3A-anatomy%2C-medicine-and-Parent/731b4084f12d359d5ae7f30a6b9cee7c35a765fe> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q961753 ;
    foaf:familyName "d'Azyr" ;
    foaf:givenName "Félix Vicq" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/felix-vicq-dazyr/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Félix_Vicq-d'Azyr> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/anne-robert-jacques-turgot/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/antoine-de-fourcroy/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/antoine-lavoisier/#person>,
