<https://culturalcartography.net/names/paul-nizan/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Paul Nizan" ;
    dcterms:description "Nizan is today remembered best for his politically-engaged fiction. Sartre and he met at school, later becoming constant companions. Aron became a friend when studying at the École Nationale Supérieure. Guterman was a colleague in the 'Philosophes' group of marxist intellectuals, founded by Lefebvre, for one of whose works Nizan wrote a foreword. Aragon and Rolland were friends as well as colleagues, and Malraux particularly close. Sartre was responsible for rescuing Nizan's reputation a decade after he'd been accused of treachery by his communist allies (including Lefebvre). It was Nizan's suggestion that led Lévi-Strauss to São Paulo, where he taught philosophy."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.cheminsdememoire.gouv.fr/en/paul-nizan>,
        <https://www.nytimes.com/1973/06/24/archives/antoine-bloye-a-novel-by-paul-nizan-introduction-by-richard-elman.html> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q723621 ;
    foaf:familyName "Nizan" ;
    foaf:givenName "Paul" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/paul-nizan/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Nizan>,
        <https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_Nizan> ;
    foaf:knows <https://culturalcartography.net/names/andre-malraux/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/claude-levi-strauss/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/georges-canguilhem/#person>,
        <https://culturalcartography.net/names/henri-lefebvre/#person>,
