<https://culturalcartography.net/names/gilbert-ryle/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Gilbert Ryle" ;
    dcterms:description "Ryle, a mainstay of British philosophy, was known for his critique of Cartesian dualism, and coined the phrase 'the ghost in the machine'. Austin was a colleague at Oxford, Ayer and Dennett (of whom he was very fond) among his students. He supervised Adorno's thesis; they had a productive relationship despite their very different philosophical heritages. Wittgenstein was a friend — they went on walking holidays together — and said Ryle was one of only two people to understand him (they later fell out, Wittgenstein calling him a charlatan). He told Strawson that he read all of Jane Austen's novels every year."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ryle/>,
        <https://www.britannica.com/biography/Gilbert-Ryle> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q313386 ;
    foaf:familyName "Ryle" ;
    foaf:givenName "Gilbert" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/gilbert-ryle/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gilbert_Ryle> ;
