<https://culturalcartography.net/names/kenneth-patchen/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Kenneth Patchen" ;
    dcterms:description "Miller described his first impression of Patchen as \"a sort of sincere assassin\" — they became close friends. Ferlinghetti and Rexroth were poet-friends amid the San Francisco bohemian scene. He joined Mingus in a poetry/jazz collaboration, and Cage on a radio play ('The City Wears A Slouch Hat'). Cummings, a neighbour in Greenwich Village, became a lasting friend despite Patchen's initial awe of him, and was one of many eminent poets who set up a fund to pay for his spinal surgery costs. Nin, who saw early drafts of his work, called him \"a man who reads newspapers and has nightmares.\""@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.poets.org/poet.php/prmPID/23> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q595662 ;
    foaf:familyName "Patchen" ;
    foaf:givenName "Kenneth" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/kenneth-patchen/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenneth_Patchen> ;
