<https://culturalcartography.net/names/bernard-germain-de-lacepede/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Bernard Germain de Lacépède" ;
    dcterms:description "The aristocratic Lacépède is mostly known for his continuation, after Buffon's death, of his great descriptive 'Natural History'. Gluck complimented the 20-year-old on his unperformed opera. Buffon befriended him after reading some of his writings, taught him (as did Daubenton), and regarded him as his protégé and chosen successor. Cuvier was a close colleague, as were Monge, Berthollet, Chaptal and Laplace. Geoffroy corresponded from Egypt, and took over his position when he fled the Revolution. Lalande was a Masonic colleague; Jefferson corresponded about the possibility of trade-routes and live mammoths."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.britannica.com/biography/Etienne-de-La-Ville-sur-Illon-comte-de-Lacepede> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q313363 ;
    skos:altLabel "Bernard Germain de La Cépède" ;
