<https://culturalcartography.net/names/hugh-macdiarmid/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Hugh MacDiarmid" ;
    dcterms:description "Gibbon was a close friend: they collaborated on a polemical book about Scotland. Trocchi had a famous spat with him at a conference organised by Calder, MacDiarmid calling him \"cosmopolitan scum\", but they wrote to one another and became unlikely friends. Pound and he met only once, but got on very well; MacDiarmid said that he loved Pound. Buchan was a colleague, Yeats was met in Ireland, while Eliot turned down as uncommercial a long poem MacDiarmid submitted for publication. MacDiarmid described the 18-year-old Finlay as \"very delightful\", but later turned against him."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.bbc.co.uk/programmes/profiles/60m5vt5DBNq4q02PQtpFqzq/hugh-macdiarmid>,
        <https://www.britannica.com/biography/Hugh-MacDiarmid>,
