<https://culturalcartography.net/names/simeon-denis-poisson/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Siméon-Denis Poisson" ;
    dcterms:description "At eighteen, Poisson caught Legendre's attention with a paper on finite differences (he later gave Legendre's funeral oration, saying he had only wanted to be spoken about in terms of his work). Poisson was a student, and later close friend, of both Laplace and Lagrange, and a member of the influential Société d'Arcueil that Laplace and Berthollet founded. Galois, barely twenty, sent Poisson a paper on equation theory, which Poisson thought unclear. Arago, another société d'Arcueil member, wrote Poisson's biography, reporting his words that he was good for only two things — doing mathematics, and teaching it."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www-groups.dcs.st-and.ac.uk/~history/Biographies/Poisson.html>,
        <https://www.britannica.com/biography/Simeon-Denis-Poisson> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q190772 ;
    foaf:familyName "Poisson" ;
    foaf:givenName "Siméon-Denis" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/simeon-denis-poisson/>,
