<https://culturalcartography.net/names/samuel-thomas-von-sommerring/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Samuel Thomas von Sömmerring" ;
    dcterms:description "Blumenbach (a real friend) and Lichtenberg taught Sömmerring; Heyne was another long-term Göttingen connection. His work on cranial nerves as a 23-year-old remains valid, two centuries later. Sömmerring met Forster (becoming close friends) and Hunter in London, and Camper in Friesland. Forster, now in Kassel, arranged a professorship in anatomy for him there (they later fell out as neighbours in Mainz). Sömmerring got to know his lifelong correspondent Goethe through a shared interest in comparative anatomy, and famously sent him an elephant's skull. Kant, ignoring its philosophical discrepancies, wrote an afterword for his essay on the soul."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://ethw.org/Samuel_Thomas_von_Sömmerring> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q132197 ;
    skos:altLabel "Samuel Thomas von Soemmerring" ;
    foaf:familyName "Sömmerring" ;
