<https://culturalcartography.net/names/wieland-herzfelde/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Wieland Herzfelde" ;
    dcterms:description "Herzfelde collaborated extensively with his more celebrated brother, Heartfield. Together with Grosz, they founded a magazine 'die Pleite.' Huelsenbeck and Baader were among fellow activists in the Berlin Dada scene from 1918. Through his Malik-Verlag (and encouraged by colleagues Piscator and Lasker-Schüler – she inspired his nom de plume as well as the business name), Herzfelde published work of Grosz and Brecht among others. Canetti worked as a translator for him. Brecht, Döblin, Mann, Feuchtwanger, Bloch and Herzfelde co-founded an anti-fascist publishing company while in exile in the U.S., before his return to Germany in 1949."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www.moma.org/s/ge/collection_ge/artist/artist_id-11705_role-3.html> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q2568679 ;
