<https://culturalcartography.net/names/joseph-louis-lagrange/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Joseph Louis Lagrange" ;
    dcterms:description "At 19, Lagrange wrote to Euler, proposing a new form of calculus. Poisson and he spurred each other on to refine planetary mathematics. D'Alembert supported him, Laplace was a mathematical correspondent and rival, and Lavoisier intervened to ensure that Lagrange, Italian-born, would not meet hostility in post-revolutionary Paris (Lavoisier was himself beheaded a few months later). Lagrange taught Fourier and encouraged Cauchy in his studies. A paper Germain sent led her to become (as a woman and outsider) his protégée. Lambert was a close friend, and Monge visited him when he was dying."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://www-history.mcs.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Lagrange.html>,
        <https://www.famousscientists.org/joseph-louis-lagrange/> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q80222 ;
    foaf:familyName "Lagrange" ;
    foaf:givenName "Joseph Louis" ;
    foaf:isPrimaryTopicOf <https://culturalcartography.net/names/joseph-louis-lagrange/>,
        <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Louis_Lagrange> ;
