<https://culturalcartography.net/names/paul-valery/#person> a foaf:Person ;
    rdfs:label "Paul Valéry" ;
    dcterms:description "Einstein, Bohr, Bergson and de Broglie were personal friends and correspondents. Louÿs introduced both Mallarmé and Gide to him: he became Mallarmé's protégé, regularly attending his literary evenings. He met Curie in Spain, Rilke in Switzerland, and Conrad when inaugurating a plaque marking Verlaine's London lodgings. Degas introduced him to his future wife, Breton asked him to be his best man, Honegger collaborated on an opera-ballet, and Tailleferre on a cantata. Stravinsky felt he thought too much about thought. Later in life, Gide persuaded him to publish the poetry he’d written years earlier."@en ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://authorscalendar.info/pvalery.htm>,
        <https://www.poets.org/poetsorg/poet/paul-valéry> ;
    owl:sameAs wd:Q200639 ;
    skos:editorialNote "Removed the link between Michael Faraday and Paul Valéry: Faraday died in 1867; Valéry was born in 1871. Neither bio mentions the other"@en ;
    foaf:familyName "Valéry" ;
    foaf:givenName "Paul" ;
